OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026", "title": "Temperedness at ramified places for globally generic exceptional groups", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/ramified-ramanujan.pdf", "family": "014", "family_title": "Restricted geometric Langlands, global Arthur enhancements, and generic Ramanujan", "abstract_html": "We prove the generalized Ramanujan conjecture for globally generic cuspidal automorphic representations of split connected adjoint exceptional groups over global function fields. Every local component is tempered, without restrictions on characteristic or ramification depth. The proof extends the unramified Ramanujan theorem of a companion paper to all ramified places.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(\\pi\\) is globally generic, then \\(\\pi_v\\) is tempered for every place \\(v\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X\\) be a smooth projective geometrically connected curve over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), let \\(F=\\mathbb F_q(X)\\), and let \\(\\mathbb A_F\\) be the adele ring of \\(F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G/F\\) be split, connected, adjoint, and absolutely simple, of type \\(G_2,F_4,E_6,E_7\\), or \\(E_8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a split Borel subgroup \\(B=TN\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi=\\bigotimes_v'\\pi_v\\) be a complex cuspidal automorphic representation of \\(G(\\mathbb A_F)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The representation \\(\\pi\\) is globally generic if some vector has a nonzero Whittaker coefficient\n  \\[\n  \\int_{N(F)\\backslash N(\\mathbb A_F)}f(ng)\\psi(n)^{-1}\\,dn,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\psi\\) is nontrivial in every simple-root coordinate.</p><p class=\"li\">Temperedness means that the unitary local representation is weakly contained in the regular representation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\pi\\) is globally generic, then \\(\\pi_v\\) is tempered for every place \\(v\\). There is no restriction on the characteristic of \\(F\\), the ramification depth, or the local representation type.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a nonzero global Whittaker functional for \\(\\pi\\). Every local component of its character is nondegenerate: on a simple root group, an automorphic additive character is indexed under adelic additive duality by a nonzero element of \\(F\\), and is therefore nontrivial at every place.</p><p>Evaluate the global functional on a pure tensor where it is nonzero, and then vary the factor at \\(v\\). This gives a nonzero local Whittaker functional on \\(\\pi_v\\). Thus every \\(\\pi_v\\) is generic.</p><p>Use the companion result, stated in the paper as original Theorem \\(6.1\\), unramified generalized Ramanujan: if \\(G/F\\) is split, connected, adjoint, and absolutely simple, and a complex cuspidal automorphic representation has a generic unramified component, then all its unramified components are tempered [21, Corollary \\(1.2\\)]. Almost every \\(\\pi_v\\) is unramified, so this result applies.</p><p>Choose an occurring global parameter \\(\\Sigma\\) as in Proposition \\(2\\); \\(G\\) has trivial center. On a sufficiently small nonempty open subset \\(X^\\circ\\subset X\\), the adjoint local system\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ\\Sigma\n\\]\nis lisse, and its Frobenius classes are the adjoints of the normalized Satake parameters. Temperedness at these places says that every such eigenvalue has absolute value \\(1\\). Hence \\(\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ\\Sigma\\) is pointwise \\(\\iota\\)-pure of weight zero.</p><p>Fix any place \\(v\\), removing it from \\(X^\\circ\\) if necessary. Deligne's local-monodromy theorem for curves [9, Théorème \\(1.8.4\\)] shows that the local Weil–Deligne representation of this adjoint sheaf is pure of weight zero: on the \\(j\\)-th monodromy-graded piece, Frobenius eigenvalues have absolute value \\(Q_v^{j/2}\\). The theorem applies in the fixed-\\(\\iota\\) form to pointwise pure lisse sheaves on open curves. Frobenius semisimplification does not change those eigenvalues.</p><p>Let \\((r_v,N_v)\\) be the dual-group-valued pair of the local restriction of \\(\\Sigma\\). Proposition \\(2\\) identifies \\(r_v\\), up to conjugacy, with \\(\\rho_{\\pi_v}\\). Its adjoint completion is pure of weight zero.</p><p>The required local implication follows directly from Propositions \\(4\\), \\(5\\), and \\(7\\), as follows. Suppose \\(\\tau=\\pi_v\\) were not tempered. By the Langlands classification, it would be the generic Langlands quotient of\n\\[\nI_P^G(\\sigma_\\nu),\n\\]\nwhere \\(P=MR\\) is proper, \\(\\sigma\\) is tempered, and \\(\\nu\\) lies in the open positive chamber. There is no real central twisting direction for an adjoint group.</p><p>Proposition \\(7\\) gives a completion \\((r,N_M)\\), realized in the fixed dual Levi, with \\(r\\simeq\\rho_\\tau\\) and adjoint \\(L\\)-factor regular at \\(1\\). Transport the completion obtained from the global parameter to \\((r,N_*)\\). Its adjoint is pure, so Proposition \\(4\\) makes its adjoint \\(L\\)-factor regular at \\(1\\). Proposition \\(5\\) conjugates \\(N_M\\) to \\(N_*\\) by the centralizer of \\(r\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ(r,N_M)\n\\]\nis pure of weight zero.</p><p>Since \\(r(W_{F_v})\\subseteq\\widehat M\\) and \\(N_M\\in\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat M)\\), the positive dual nilradical \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\\) is a Weil–Deligne direct summand. Proposition \\(4\\) gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\det(r(\\mathrm{Fr})\\mid\\widehat{\\mathfrak n})\\right|=1.\n\\]</p><p>On each central-weight summand \\(V_b\\), the untwisted tempered parameter has Weil determinant of absolute value \\(1\\). Its \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) factor has determinant \\(1\\), because \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) has no nontrivial algebraic characters. Twisting by \\(\\nu\\) multiplies each eigenvalue on \\(V_b\\) by \\(Q_v^{-b(\\nu)}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\left|\\det(r(\\mathrm{Fr})\\mid\\widehat{\\mathfrak n})\\right|\n=\nQ_v^{-\\sum_b b(\\nu)\\dim V_b}\n&lt;1,\n\\]\nbecause \\(P\\) is proper and every \\(b(\\nu)&gt;0\\). This contradiction proves that \\(\\pi_v\\) is tempered.</p><p>Finally, the local representations are unitarizable because \\(\\pi\\) occurs in the cuspidal \\(L^2\\)-spectrum of the adjoint group. Temperedness in the nonarchimedean Langlands classification is equivalent to weak containment of this unitary realization in the regular representation, as in Waldspurger [27]. This is the meaning asserted in the theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem \\(5.2\\), the pure completion criterion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(\\rho_\\tau\\) admits a Weil–Deligne completion \\((\\rho_\\tau,N_*)\\) whose adjoint representation is pure of weight zero, then \\(\\tau\\) is tempered.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a nonarchimedean local field of positive characteristic, with residue field of cardinality \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G/k\\) be split, adjoint, and absolutely simple, and let \\(\\widehat G\\) be its complex dual group.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\tau\\) be an irreducible generic smooth representation of \\(G(k)\\). Here genericity means the existence of a nonzero Whittaker functional for a smooth unitary character of a maximal unipotent subgroup that is nontrivial on every simple root group.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\rho_\\tau:W_k\\to\\widehat G(\\mathbb C)\\) for its semisimple local parameter.</p><p class=\"li\">Use geometric Frobenius \\(\\mathrm{Fr}\\), so \\(|\\mathrm{Fr}|=Q^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Frobenius-semisimple Weil–Deligne completion of \\(\\rho_\\tau\\) is a pair \\((\\rho_\\tau,N_*)\\), with finite inertia image and nilpotent \\(N_*\\in\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat G)\\), satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Ad}(\\rho_\\tau(w))N_*=|w|N_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a nilpotent operator \\(N\\) on a vector space \\(V\\), its monodromy filtration centered at zero is the unique increasing filtration \\(M_\\bullet V\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  N(M_jV)\\subset M_{j-2}V,\\qquad\n  N^j:\\operatorname{Gr}^M_jV\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\operatorname{Gr}^M_{-j}V\n  \\quad(j\\geq0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A Weil–Deligne representation is pure of weight zero, with respect to the fixed complex realization of its coefficients, if the Frobenius eigenvalues on \\(\\operatorname{Gr}^M_jV\\) have absolute value \\(Q^{j/2}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\rho_\\tau\\) admits a Weil–Deligne completion \\((\\rho_\\tau,N_*)\\) whose adjoint representation is pure of weight zero, then \\(\\tau\\) is tempered.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume that \\(\\tau\\) is not tempered. By the Langlands classification, it has the description in Proposition \\(7\\), with \\(P=MR\\) proper and \\(\\nu\\) strictly positive. There is no real central twisting direction for an adjoint group.</p><p>Let \\((r,N_M)\\) be the completion of Proposition \\(7\\), retaining its realization in the fixed dual Levi \\(\\widehat M\\). Transport the assumed completion by conjugacy to a pair \\((r,N_*)\\) over the same Weil parameter. Its adjoint representation remains pure.</p><p>By Proposition \\(4\\), the adjoint \\(L\\)-factor of \\((r,N_*)\\) is regular at \\(1\\). The adjoint \\(L\\)-factor of \\((r,N_M)\\) is regular at \\(1\\) by Proposition \\(7\\). Proposition \\(5\\) therefore conjugates \\(N_M\\) to \\(N_*\\) by the centralizer of \\(r\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ(r,N_M)\n\\]\nis pure of weight zero.</p><p>The Weil image lies in \\(\\widehat M\\), and \\(N_M\\) lies in \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat M)\\). Thus \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\\) is a Weil–Deligne direct summand of the adjoint representation. Proposition \\(4\\) makes this summand pure of weight zero and gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\det(r(\\mathrm{Fr})\\mid\\widehat{\\mathfrak n})\\right|=1.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]</p><p>Compute the same determinant before and after the twist. On every central-weight summand \\(V_b\\), the untwisted tempered parameter has Weil determinant of absolute value \\(1\\). Its \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) factor has determinant \\(1\\), since \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) has no nontrivial algebraic characters. Thus the untwisted Weil–Deligne Frobenius determinant has absolute value \\(1\\) on \\(V_b\\).</p><p>Twisting by \\(\\nu\\) multiplies each eigenvalue on \\(V_b\\) by \\(Q^{-b(\\nu)}\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\left|\\det(r(\\mathrm{Fr})\\mid\\widehat{\\mathfrak n})\\right|\n=\nQ^{-\\sum_b b(\\nu)\\dim V_b}\n&lt;1.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nThe inequality is strict because \\(P\\) is proper and every \\(b(\\nu)&gt;0\\). This contradicts \\((5.1)\\), proving that \\(\\tau\\) is tempered.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The center of \\(H\\) is split and connected.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a nonarchimedean local field of positive characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G/k\\) be split, adjoint, and absolutely simple, with split maximal torus \\(T\\), fixed Borel subgroup, and fixed positive roots.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a standard Levi subgroup of \\(G\\), and let \\(U_H\\) be its maximal unipotent subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">A nondegenerate unitary character of \\(U_H(k)\\) is a smooth unitary character nontrivial on every simple root group of \\(H\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The center of \\(H\\) is split and connected. Every smooth character of \\(U_H(k)\\) is trivial on the nonsimple positive root groups. The nondegenerate unitary characters of \\(U_H(k)\\) form a single \\(T(k)\\)-orbit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Because \\(G\\) is adjoint, its simple roots are a \\(\\mathbb Z\\)-basis of \\(X^*(T)\\). The center of \\(H\\) is the simultaneous kernel of a subset of these coordinate characters, hence is a split torus. The same basis allows arbitrary independent rescaling of the simple-root coordinates belonging to \\(H\\). Once triviality on nonsimple root groups is known, additive self-duality of \\(k\\) proves the orbit assertion.</p><p>To prove that triviality, a nonsimple positive root may be written as a sum of two positive roots. The full root subsystem in their span reduces the question to \\(A_2,B_2\\), or \\(G_2\\), with its induced positive system.</p><p>In \\(A_2\\), the usual commutator parametrizes the nonsimple root group. For \\(B_2\\) or \\(G_2\\), write \\(a,b\\) for the short and long simple roots. In \\(G_2\\), first kill the highest root group \\(U_{3a+2b}\\) using \\([U_b,U_{3a+b}]\\), whose coefficient is \\(\\pm1\\). Modulo that group, the commutator \\([x_a(u),x_b(t)]\\) has factors\n\\[\nx_{ja+b}(\\epsilon_j u^jt),\\qquad \\epsilon_j\\in\\{1,-1\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\leq j\\leq2\\) in \\(B_2\\) and \\(1\\leq j\\leq3\\) in \\(G_2\\).</p><p>Fix a nontrivial additive character \\(\\psi\\) of \\(k\\), and write the restriction of the given character to \\(U_{ja+b}\\) as\n\\[\nx_{ja+b}(t)\\longmapsto\\psi(c_jt).\n\\]\nIt kills commutators, so\n\\[\n\\psi\\left(t\\sum_j\\epsilon_jc_ju^j\\right)=1\n\\qquad\\text{for every }u,t\\in k.\n\\]\nVarying \\(t\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\epsilon_jc_ju^j=0\n\\qquad\\text{for every }u\\in k.\n\\]\nSince \\(k\\) is infinite, all \\(c_j\\) vanish. This polynomial argument uses no division by \\(2\\) or \\(3\\), and remains valid in those characteristics.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The cited parameter inputs apply to the complex representations under consideration, with the coefficient and occurrence interpretation described in Lemma \\(2.1\\) below.", "statement": "<p>Parameter inputs and their consequences: original Theorems \\(2.2\\), \\(2.3\\), and Lemma \\(2.4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Locally, let \\(k\\) be a nonarchimedean local field of positive characteristic, let \\(G/k\\) be split, adjoint, and absolutely simple, and let \\(H\\) be \\(G\\) or a standard Levi subgroup of \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\ell\\neq\\operatorname{char}(k)\\) and an isomorphism\n  \\[\n  \\iota:\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C.\n  \\]\n  Absolute values of coefficients are taken after \\(\\iota\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use geometric Frobenius, local class field theory sending a uniformizer to geometric Frobenius, and compatible positive real square roots in normalized induction and the parameter theorems. Thus twisting a representation by \\(|\\chi|^s\\) gives the corresponding \\(|\\cdot|^s\\)-twist of its parameter.</p><p class=\"li\">Globally, let \\(K\\) be a function field over a finite field, let \\(H/K\\) be one of these split groups, and let \\(\\Pi\\) be a cuspidal automorphic representation with finite-order central character.</p><p class=\"li\">The cited parameter theorem of Lafforgue and Genestier–Lafforgue, stated as original Theorem \\(2.2\\), supplies:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{ll}\n  \\text{(i)}&amp;\n  \\text{A semisimple parameter }\\rho_\\sigma:W_k\\to\\widehat H\n  \\text{ for every irreducible smooth local }\\sigma,\\\\\n  &amp;\\text{with finite inertia image and compatibility with normalized induction,}\\\\\n  &amp;\\text{tori, local class field theory, group isomorphisms, central characters,}\\\\\n  &amp;\\text{and twists through algebraic characters;}\\\\{}[2mm]\n  \\text{(ii)}&amp;\n  \\text{An occurring semisimple global excursion parameter }\\Sigma_\\Pi,\\\\\n  &amp;\\text{defined over a finite extension of }\\mathbb Q_\\ell\n  \\text{ and matching normalized Satake parameters}\\\\\n  &amp;\\text{away from a finite set;}\\\\{}[2mm]\n  \\text{(iii)}&amp;\n  (\\Sigma_\\Pi|_{W_{K_v}})^{\\mathrm{ss}}\n  \\text{ is conjugate to }\\rho_{\\Pi_v}\\text{ at every place }v.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A tempered completion is one arising from a homomorphism\n  \\[\n  \\phi:W_k\\times\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb C)\\to\\widehat H(\\mathbb C)\n  \\]\n  that is algebraic on \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) and has bounded Weil image, through\n  \\[\n  r(w)=\\phi\\left(w,\n  \\begin{pmatrix}|w|^{1/2}&amp;0\\\\0&amp;|w|^{-1/2}\\end{pmatrix}\\right),\n  \\qquad\n  N=d\\phi\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n  \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cited parameter inputs apply to the complex representations under consideration, with the coefficient and occurrence interpretation described in Lemma \\(2.1\\) below. Moreover:</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\sigma\\) is tempered on a standard Levi subgroup \\(M\\) of \\(G\\), then \\(\\rho_\\sigma\\) has a tempered completion \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_M)\\), in every positive characteristic.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(\\Sigma\\) is a continuous \\(\\ell\\)-adic representation of a local decomposition group into \\(\\widehat H\\), defined over a finite extension of \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\), and \\((r,N)\\) is its Frobenius-semisimplified Weil–Deligne pair, then \\(r\\) is conjugate to \\((\\Sigma|_{W_k})^{\\mathrm{ss}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consequently, the Weil part of the local Weil–Deligne pair of an occurring global parameter at \\(v\\) is conjugate to \\(\\rho_{\\Pi_v}\\). No identification of monodromy operators is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lafforgue [17, Théorème \\(0.1\\)] for the global parameter theorem and Genestier–Lafforgue [14, Théorème \\(0.1\\) and Remarque \\(0.2\\)] for the local statements, including representations without an integrality condition. For central-character compatibility, apply functoriality for homomorphisms with normal image to the inclusion of the central split torus.</p><p>Lemma \\(2.1\\) gives the coefficient and occurrence interpretation. Lemma \\(2.2\\) gives the asserted tempered completions. Lemma \\(2.3\\), combined with semisimple local-global compatibility, identifies the Weil part of each global restriction with the corresponding semisimple local parameter. The global restriction and a tempered local completion may still retain distinct monodromy operators.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The finite-extension formulations of the parameter theorems can be used for the complex representations in Proposition \\(2\\).", "statement": "<p>Coefficient and occurrence interpretation from Section \\(2.3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the local and global groups and the coefficient identification \\(\\iota\\) of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Globally, assume the central character of \\(\\Pi\\) has finite order.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The finite-extension formulations of the parameter theorems can be used for the complex representations in Proposition \\(2\\). An irreducible automorphic Hecke module can be placed in an occurring excursion summand without requiring uniqueness of its excursion parameter.</p>", "proof": "<p>A finite-order central character allows a cocompact central lattice in its kernel. Fixed-level cusp spaces modulo that lattice are finite-dimensional and commute with coefficient extension. Project an irreducible Hecke module of level invariants to any excursion summand where its projection is nonzero. That projection is injective, so it supplies the required occurrence. Uniqueness of the excursion parameter of \\(\\Pi\\) is unnecessary. For an adjoint group, no central lattice is needed.</p><p>Locally, after transport by \\(\\iota\\), irreducibles may be realized over a finite extension of \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\). Indeed, a fixed-compact-open Hecke algebra over \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\) is of finite type by Dat–Helm–Kurinczuk–Moss [5, Theorem \\(1.1\\)], so its finite-dimensional simple module over \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\) descends to such an extension.</p><p>The corresponding irreducible representation also descends: form induction from this Hecke module and quotient by the largest subrepresentation with zero invariants under that compact open subgroup. The latter subrepresentation is characterized by vanishing of every averaged translate, a condition compatible with scalar extension. This permits use of the finite-extension formulations of the parameter theorems.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Theorem \\(2.3\\), in the cited tempered form", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The parameter \\(\\rho_\\sigma\\) admits a tempered completion \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_M)\\) of the form \\((2.3)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be a standard Levi subgroup of the split adjoint absolutely simple group \\(G/k\\) in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) be a tempered representation of \\(M(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the semisimple parameter \\(\\rho_\\sigma\\) supplied by the cited parameter theorem.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The parameter \\(\\rho_\\sigma\\) admits a tempered completion \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_M)\\) of the form \\((2.3)\\). This holds in every positive characteristic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Gan–Harris–Sawin, with the appendix by Beuzart-Plessis [11, Theorem \\(1.2\\) and Corollary \\(1.3\\)], give an essentially tempered completion for arbitrary connected reductive groups over local function fields. Thus its Weil image is bounded modulo the center.</p><p>Since \\(\\sigma\\) is tempered, its central character is unitary. By the central-character compatibility in original Theorem \\(2.2\\), the dual morphism\n\\[\n\\widehat M\\longrightarrow\\widehat{Z(M)}\n\\]\nhas bounded image on the parameter. Root data show that its characters span the rational character space of the abelian quotient of \\(\\widehat M\\). Moreover, the \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) factor dies in that quotient. Hence the Weil image of the completion is bounded in the abelian quotient as well.</p><p>The map from \\(\\widehat M\\) to the product of its adjoint and abelian quotients has finite kernel. Boundedness in these two quotients therefore gives boundedness in \\(\\widehat M\\) itself.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma \\(2.4\\)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The Weil parameter \\(r\\) is conjugate to the semisimplification of \\(\\Sigma|_{W_k}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) and \\(\\widehat H\\) be as in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Sigma\\) be a continuous \\(\\ell\\)-adic representation of a local decomposition group into \\(\\widehat H\\), defined over a finite extension of \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((r,N)\\) be its Frobenius-semisimplified Weil–Deligne pair.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Weil parameter \\(r\\) is conjugate to the semisimplification of \\(\\Sigma|_{W_k}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Before Frobenius semisimplification, write the pair as \\((r_0,N)\\), with \\(r_0(I_k)\\) finite. The construction takes place in \\(\\widehat H\\): in a faithful linear representation, the unipotent logarithm lies in its Lie algebra, and \\(\\Sigma\\) on the Weil group is obtained from \\(r_0\\) by multiplying by the appropriate exponentials of \\(N\\).</p><p>Write\n\\[\nr_0(\\mathrm{Fr})=hu\n\\]\nfor its commuting semisimple and unipotent parts. Some positive power of \\(r_0(\\mathrm{Fr})\\) centralizes the finite inertia image. Jordan decomposition in that centralizer shows that a power of \\(u\\), and hence \\(u\\), centralizes it. The relation\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}(hu)N=Q^{-1}N\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}(h)N=Q^{-1}N,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Ad}(u)N=N.\n\\]\nReplacing \\(hu\\) by \\(h\\) thus defines a Weil parameter \\(r\\) with the same finite inertia. The identity component of the Zariski closure of \\(r(W_k)\\) is toral, so \\(r\\) is semisimple.</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be the identity component of the Zariski closure of the cyclic group generated by \\(h\\). It is a torus centralizing \\(r_0(I_k)\\) and \\(u\\). If \\(N\\neq0\\), its weight on the line \\(\\mathbb CN\\) is nontrivial, because \\(Q^{-1}\\) is not a root of unity. A cocharacter of \\(S\\) can therefore contract \\(N\\) to zero while fixing \\(r_0\\). This realizes removal of the monodromy exponentials as a conjugation limit in \\(\\widehat H\\).</p><p>Next, the centralizer of \\(r\\) has reductive identity component and contains the unipotent element \\(u\\). A cocharacter in that centralizer contracts \\(u\\) to the identity. These two limits preserve semisimplification and leave precisely \\(r\\). Finally, density of the Weil group in the decomposition group gives the same Zariski closure for both images.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "a local-factor ratio independent of monodromy", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One has \\( \\frac{L(1-s,(r,N)^\\vee)}{L(s,(r,N))} \\doteq \\frac{L(1-s,(r,N')^\\vee)}{L(s,(r,N'))}. \\) More precisely, put \\( W=V^{r(I_k)},\\qquad K=\\ker(N|_W),\\qquad F=r(\\mathrm{Fr}), \\) and let \\(R_N(s)\\) denote the ratio …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) have residue cardinality \\(Q\\), and use geometric Frobenius \\(\\mathrm{Fr}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\) be a Frobenius-semisimple Weil representation on a finite-dimensional complex vector space \\(V\\), with finite inertia image.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N,N'\\) be nilpotent operators such that\n  \\[\n  r(w)Nr(w)^{-1}=|w|N,\\qquad\n  r(w)N'r(w)^{-1}=|w|N'.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  L(s,(r,N))\n  =\n  \\det\\left(1-Q^{-s}r(\\mathrm{Fr})\n       \\mid(\\ker N)^{r(I_k)}\\right)^{-1}.\n  \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]\n  This is a reciprocal polynomial in \\(Q^{-s}\\), with constant term \\(1\\), and has no zeros.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(f\\doteq g\\) for equality up to a nonzero constant times a monomial in exponential unramified-twist coordinates. Finite covers of twist tori are allowed. In one variable the omitted factor has the form \\(cQ^{-as}\\), and is holomorphic and nowhere zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n\\frac{L(1-s,(r,N)^\\vee)}{L(s,(r,N))}\n\\doteq\n\\frac{L(1-s,(r,N')^\\vee)}{L(s,(r,N'))}.\n\\]\nMore precisely, put\n\\[\nW=V^{r(I_k)},\\qquad K=\\ker(N|_W),\\qquad F=r(\\mathrm{Fr}),\n\\]\nand let \\(R_N(s)\\) denote the ratio on the left. Then\n\\[\n\\frac{R_0(s)}{R_N(s)}\n=\n\\det\\left(-Q^{-s}F\\mid W/K\\right).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The operator \\(N\\) commutes with inertia and satisfies\n\\[\nFN=Q^{-1}NF.\n\\]\nThus \\(N\\) identifies \\(W/K\\) with \\(NW\\), multiplying Frobenius eigenvalues by \\(Q^{-1}\\).</p><p>Averaging over the finite inertia image identifies\n\\[\n(V^\\vee)^{r^\\vee(I_k)}\\simeq W^\\vee.\n\\]\nThe dual monodromy operator is \\(-N^t\\). On \\(W^\\vee\\), its kernel is the annihilator of \\(NW\\), so restriction of functionals gives the Frobenius-equivariant isomorphism\n\\[\nW^\\vee/\\ker(-N^t)\\simeq(NW)^\\vee.\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, if \\(c\\) is a Frobenius eigenvalue on \\(W/K\\), the corresponding eigenvalue on this dual quotient is \\(Qc^{-1}\\). Writing \\(x=Q^{-s}\\) and comparing the determinants for \\(N\\) and \\(0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{R_0(s)}{R_N(s)}\n=\n\\prod_c\\frac{1-xc}{1-Q^{-1}x^{-1}(Qc^{-1})}\n=\n\\prod_c(-xc),\n\\]\nwhere the eigenvalues are counted with multiplicity.</p><p>This proves \\((3.1)\\) as an identity of rational functions in \\(x\\). Its right-hand side is a nonzero constant times a monomial. Comparison through \\(N=0\\) proves the assertion for \\(N\\) and \\(N'\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "consequences of purity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The function \\(L(s,(r,N))\\) is holomorphic and nonzero for \\(\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0\\), and \\( |\\det r(\\mathrm{Fr})|=1. \\) Every Weil–Deligne direct summand of \\((r,N)\\) is also pure of weight zero and satisfies these …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) have residue cardinality \\(Q\\), and use geometric Frobenius.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((r,N)\\) be a finite-dimensional Frobenius-semisimple Weil–Deligne representation, with finite inertia image.</p><p class=\"li\">Its monodromy filtration \\(M_\\bullet V\\) is centered at zero:\n  \\[\n  N(M_jV)\\subset M_{j-2}V,\\qquad\n  N^j:\\operatorname{Gr}^M_jV\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\operatorname{Gr}^M_{-j}V.\n  \\]\n  On a Jordan chain \\(v,Nv,\\ldots,N^dv\\), the successive degrees are \\(d,d-2,\\ldots,-d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\((r,N)\\) is pure of weight zero: the Frobenius eigenvalues on \\(\\operatorname{Gr}^M_jV\\) have absolute value \\(Q^{j/2}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the local \\(L\\)-factor defined in Proposition \\(3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(L(s,(r,N))\\) is holomorphic and nonzero for \\(\\operatorname{Re}(s)&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\n|\\det r(\\mathrm{Fr})|=1.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nEvery Weil–Deligne direct summand of \\((r,N)\\) is also pure of weight zero and satisfies these conclusions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Jordan-chain description constructs the monodromy filtration and shows that it commutes with direct sums. Its uniqueness shows that the Weil action preserves it, since conjugation by \\(r(w)\\) multiplies \\(N\\) by the nonzero scalar \\(|w|\\).</p><p>The bottom vector of a Jordan chain of length \\(d+1\\) has monodromy degree \\(-d\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\ker N\\subset M_0V.\n\\]\nPurity implies that all Frobenius eigenvalues on \\(M_0V\\), and therefore on \\((\\ker N)^{I_k}\\), have absolute value at most \\(1\\). If \\(\\operatorname{Re}(s)&gt;0\\), every factor \\(1-Q^{-s}c\\) in the defining determinant is nonzero. The asserted holomorphy and nonvanishing follow.</p><p>The defining isomorphisms of the monodromy filtration give\n\\[\n\\dim\\operatorname{Gr}^M_jV=\\dim\\operatorname{Gr}^M_{-j}V.\n\\]\nTaking the determinant on the graded spaces therefore gives\n\\[\n|\\det r(\\mathrm{Fr})|\n=\nQ^{\\frac12\\sum_jj\\dim\\operatorname{Gr}^M_jV}\n=\n1.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, the monodromy filtration of a direct sum is the direct sum of its monodromy filtrations. Each graded space of a Weil–Deligne summand is consequently a Frobenius-stable direct summand of the corresponding graded space of \\(V\\), and inherits its purity.</p><p>For the distinction between purity and boundedness of the Weil part, take trivial inertia on \\(V=\\mathbb Ce_1\\oplus\\mathbb Ce_2\\) and set\n\\[\nr(\\mathrm{Fr})=\n\\begin{pmatrix}Q^{-1/2}&amp;0\\\\0&amp;Q^{1/2}\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad Ne_2=e_1,\\qquad Ne_1=0.\n\\]\nThe degrees of \\(e_1,e_2\\) are \\(-1,1\\), so the representation is pure of weight zero although \\(r\\) is unbounded. It comes from the \\(L\\)-parameter trivial on \\(W_k\\) and standard on \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2(\\mathbb C)\\). Replacing \\(N\\) by \\(0\\) destroys purity and introduces a pole at \\(s=1/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "adjoint regularity determines the monodromy orbit", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(L(s,\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ(r,N))\\) is regular at \\(s=1\\), then \\(C\\cdot N\\) is open in \\(\\mathfrak e_{Q^{-1}}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widehat G\\) be a connected semisimple complex algebraic group.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r:W_k\\to\\widehat G\\) be a Frobenius-semisimple Weil parameter with finite inertia image, where \\(k\\) has residue cardinality \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\mathfrak e=\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat G)^{r(I_k)},\\qquad\n  \\mathfrak e_a\n  =\n  \\ker\\left(\\operatorname{Ad}(r(\\mathrm{Fr}))-a\\mid\\mathfrak e\\right)\n  \\quad(a\\in\\mathbb C^\\times),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  C=Z_{\\widehat G}(r(W_k)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((r,N)\\) be a Weil–Deligne parameter. Its adjoint monodromy is \\(\\operatorname{ad}(N)\\), and its adjoint \\(L\\)-factor is defined by the determinant convention of Proposition \\(3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(L(s,\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ(r,N))\\) is regular at \\(s=1\\), then \\(C\\cdot N\\) is open in \\(\\mathfrak e_{Q^{-1}}\\).</p><p>In particular, if \\(N_1,N_2\\) are monodromy operators over the same \\(r\\), and both adjoint \\(L\\)-factors are regular at \\(1\\), then some \\(c\\in C\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}(c)N_1=N_2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The Weil–Deligne relation places \\(N\\) in \\(\\mathfrak e_{Q^{-1}}\\). Regularity at \\(1\\) is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\ker(\\operatorname{ad}N)\\cap\\mathfrak e_Q=0.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nIndeed, a pole at \\(1\\) means that Frobenius has eigenvalue \\(Q\\) on\n\\[\n(\\ker\\operatorname{ad}N)^{r(I_k)}.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(B\\) be the Killing form of \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat G)\\). Its restriction to \\(\\mathfrak e\\) is nondegenerate: averaging a vector over the finite inertia image does not change its pairing with an inertia-invariant vector. A vector in \\(\\mathfrak e\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathfrak e\\) is therefore orthogonal to the entire Lie algebra, and is zero.</p><p>Frobenius preserves \\(B\\) and acts semisimply, so \\(B\\) pairs \\(\\mathfrak e_a\\) perfectly with \\(\\mathfrak e_{a^{-1}}\\). We have\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Lie}(C)=\\mathfrak e_1.\n\\]\nThe differential of the orbit map at the identity is\n\\[\n\\mathfrak e_1\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak e_{Q^{-1}},\n\\qquad Y\\longmapsto[Y,N].\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nBy invariance of \\(B\\), its transpose under these perfect pairings is\n\\[\n\\mathfrak e_Q\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak e_1,\n\\qquad X\\longmapsto[N,X].\n\\]\nIts kernel is zero by \\((3.3)\\), so \\((3.4)\\) is surjective.</p><p>Algebraic group orbits are smooth and locally closed in characteristic zero. Thus \\(C\\cdot N\\) has the dimension of \\(\\mathfrak e_{Q^{-1}}\\) and is open in that vector space. Since the vector space is irreducible, it has at most one open \\(C\\)-orbit. This proves the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "comparison of local coefficients", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an identity of meromorphic functions of the unramified twists \\( C_H(z,\\sigma) \\doteq \\prod_b \\frac{ L(1-b(z),r_b^\\vee\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma)) }{ L(b(z),r_b\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma)) }. \\) In particular …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a local function field of residue cardinality \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a split adjoint absolutely simple group under consideration, or a standard Levi subgroup of such a group.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a split maximal torus \\(T\\), a Borel subgroup \\(B=TU\\), and a nondegenerate character \\(\\psi\\) of \\(U(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P=MR\\) be a proper standard parabolic subgroup of \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All induction is normalized:\n  \\[\n  I_P^H(\\sigma_z)=\\operatorname{Ind}_{P(k)}^{H(k)}(\\sigma_z).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) be an irreducible generic representation of \\(M(k)\\), and let\n  \\[\n  z\\in\\mathfrak a^*_{M,\\mathbb C}=X^*(M)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb C.\n  \\]\n  The notation \\(\\sigma_z\\) denotes the unramified twist in which \\(s\\chi\\) acts as \\(|\\chi|^s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Whittaker characters and Weyl representatives are chosen compatibly, using Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_H,w_M\\) be the longest Weyl elements of \\(H,M\\), put \\(w=w_Hw_M\\), and let \\(P'\\) be the standard parabolic with Levi \\(wMw^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The unnormalized standard intertwiner is\n  \\[\n  A_H(z,\\sigma;w):\n  I_P^H(\\sigma_z)\\longrightarrow I_{P'}^H((w\\sigma)_{wz}).\n  \\]\n  Define the local coefficient by\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_{\\mathrm{source}}(z)\n  =\n  C_H(z,\\sigma)\\,\n  \\lambda_{\\mathrm{target}}(wz)\\circ A_H(z,\\sigma;w).\n  \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n  The same definition is used for Weyl elements carrying the simple roots of the source Levi into ambient simple roots.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widehat P=\\widehat M\\widehat R\\) be the corresponding dual parabolic. Decompose\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\mathfrak n}:=\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat R)\n  =\n  \\bigoplus_bV_b\n  \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]\n  under \\(Z(\\widehat M)^\\circ\\), and let \\(r_b\\) be the representation of \\(\\widehat M\\) on \\(V_b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Via duality, \\(X^*(M)\\) is the cocharacter lattice of \\(Z(\\widehat M)^\\circ\\). The central weight \\(b\\) pairs with \\(z\\), giving \\(b(z)\\). In particular, \\(b(\\nu)&gt;0\\) for \\(\\nu\\) in the open positive chamber for \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma)\\) be any Weil–Deligne pair in \\(\\widehat M\\) whose Weil part is the semisimple local parameter of \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the local \\(L\\)-factor convention of Proposition \\(3\\), and write \\(\\doteq\\) for equality up to a nonzero constant times a monomial in twist coordinates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an identity of meromorphic functions of the unramified twists\n\\[\nC_H(z,\\sigma)\n\\doteq\n\\prod_b\n\\frac{\nL(1-b(z),r_b^\\vee\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma))\n}{\nL(b(z),r_b\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma))\n}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nIn particular, one may take \\(N_\\sigma=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas \\(6.3\\) and \\(6.5\\) prove the maximal-parabolic comparison for arbitrary generic inducing representations.</p><p>For general \\(P=MR\\), apply Lemma \\(6.2\\) to\n\\[\nw=w_Hw_M,\\qquad L=M,\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nM_j=w_{&lt;j}Mw_{&lt;j}^{-1}.\n\\]\nAt step \\(j\\), the inducing data are transported from \\(\\sigma\\) to \\(M_j\\), and the twist is \\(w_{&lt;j}z\\). Its relative coordinate is\n\\[\ns_j(z)=\\langle w_{&lt;j}z,\\alpha_j^\\vee\\rangle.\n\\]\nThis linear function is strictly positive on the positive chamber for \\(P\\), since its transported coroot lies outside \\(M\\) in the inversion partition. In particular, it is nonconstant. The maximal-parabolic comparison is available at each step and for every such twist; directions from \\(H_j\\) contribute only units. Multiplicativity gives the product of these step formulas for \\(C_H(z,\\sigma)\\).</p><p>Use monodromy zero in these formulas. Transporting the step nilradicals back to \\(\\widehat M\\), the inversion partition identifies their direct sum with \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\\). On a step summand of degree \\(i_j\\), the twist exponent is \\(i_js_j(z)\\), exactly its central weight \\(b(z)\\) in \\((4.2)\\).</p><p>Multiplicativity of Artin \\(L\\)-factors for direct sums identifies the product of the step expressions with the right side of \\((4.3)\\) for \\(N_\\sigma=0\\). Finally, Proposition \\(3\\), applied to every \\(r_b\\), permits any Weil–Deligne completion with the same Weil part.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Whittaker lines and specialization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The twisted coinvariants of \\(I_P^H(\\sigma_z)\\) form a free rank-one \\(A\\)-module, and this description commutes with specialization of \\(z\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(H,P,M,\\sigma,z,\\psi\\), and \\(A_H\\) from Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth representation \\(V\\), write \\(V_{U,\\psi}\\) for its twisted coinvariants.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a finite cover of the unramified-twist torus on which the characters in use are algebraic, and let \\(A\\) be its Laurent polynomial coordinate ring.</p><p class=\"li\">Regard \\(\\sigma_z\\) and \\(I_P^H(\\sigma_z)\\) as smooth families over \\(A\\). Meromorphic operators are obtained by extension to the fraction field of \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The twisted coinvariants of \\(I_P^H(\\sigma_z)\\) form a free rank-one \\(A\\)-module, and this description commutes with specialization of \\(z\\). The Jacquet-integral Whittaker functionals generate its dual, with nonzero specialization at every twist. Consequently, the inverse scalar induced by \\(A_H\\) on these lines is \\(C_H(z,\\sigma)\\), up to a unit of \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The twisted Jacquet functor is exact over \\(A\\). Indeed, \\(U(k)\\) is an increasing union of compact open subgroups. On each such subgroup, twisted averaging is an idempotent, since \\(A\\) is a complex algebra. Taking the resulting filtered colimit proves exactness. The same description shows that twisted coinvariants commute with scalar extension, including specialization.</p><p>Apply this functor to the Bruhat filtration of parabolic induction. For a representative \\(x\\) of \\(W_M\\backslash W_H\\), the corresponding subquotient consists of sections with compact support modulo \\(P(k)\\) on\n\\[\nP(k)\\dot xU(k).\n\\]\nAs a \\(U(k)\\)-representation, it is compact induction from\n\\[\nU(k)\\cap\\dot x^{-1}P(k)\\dot x,\n\\]\nwith fiber action obtained from the inducing representation. Its twisted coinvariants are therefore computed on the fiber. All unramified twists and modulus characters are trivial on the unipotent groups in this calculation, so the calculation is unchanged over \\(A\\).</p><p>Every cell other than the open one has a simple root subgroup of \\(U\\) whose conjugate lies in \\(R\\). It acts trivially on the fiber and nontrivially through \\(\\psi\\), so that cell contributes no twisted coinvariants.</p><p>For completeness, take \\(x\\) minimal on the left modulo \\(W_M\\). If no simple root is sent to a positive root outside \\(M\\), every \\(x\\alpha\\), for \\(\\alpha\\) simple, is either negative or a positive Levi root. Thus the coefficients outside the Levi simple system of every \\(x\\beta\\), for \\(\\beta\\) positive, are nonpositive. In particular, \\(x\\beta\\) cannot be a positive root outside \\(M\\). It follows that \\(x^{-1}\\) sends all positive roots outside \\(M\\) to negative roots, which characterizes the open-cell representative.</p><p>On the open cell, the remaining calculation is precisely the Whittaker coinvariant space of \\(\\sigma\\), with character transported by the chosen representative. This space is one-dimensional by Whittaker uniqueness. Hence the induced family's coinvariants are its tensor product with \\(A\\).</p><p>The open-cell submodule therefore induces an isomorphism on twisted coinvariants. Its unipotent integral, applied to a fixed Whittaker functional of \\(\\sigma\\), extends uniquely through this isomorphism to an \\(A\\)-linear Whittaker functional on the entire induced family. In a convergence chamber, this functional and the usual Jacquet integral agree on the open-cell submodule, and hence everywhere. Meromorphic continuation identifies the Jacquet functional with this algebraic functional. In particular, its evaluations on algebraic sections are Laurent polynomials.</p><p>To see that specialization is nonzero, choose a vector on which the fixed Whittaker functional of \\(\\sigma\\) is \\(1\\), and a compactly supported function in the open-cell unipotent coordinates whose \\(\\psi\\)-weighted integral is \\(1\\). The resulting algebraic section has Whittaker value \\(1\\) at every twist. Thus the regular functional never specializes to zero and generates the dual coinvariant line.</p><p>Equation \\((4.1)\\) identifies the local coefficient with the inverse scalar on these free lines. Two generators of a free rank-one Laurent polynomial module differ by a unit, hence by a nonzero constant times a monomial. Haar measures and compatible Weyl representatives change the formula only by such units. Torus conjugation of the Whittaker character also identifies these lines over \\(A\\) and commutes with the intertwining calculation, with the same consequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "elementary factorization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a length-additive factorization \\( v=v_t\\cdots v_1 \\) such that, with \\( v_{<j}=v_{j-1}\\cdots v_1,\\qquad L_j=v_{<j}Lv_{<j}^{-1}, \\) the Levi subgroup \\(L_j\\) is standard and maximal proper in a standard Levi …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be as in Proposition \\(6\\), with positive roots \\(\\Phi_H^+\\) and simple roots \\(\\Delta_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be a standard Levi subgroup, with simple roots \\(\\Delta_L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v\\in W_H\\) satisfy \\(v\\Delta_L\\subseteq\\Delta_H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Inv}(v)\n  =\n  \\{\\beta\\in\\Phi_H^+:v\\beta\\in-\\Phi_H^+\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a length-additive factorization\n\\[\nv=v_t\\cdots v_1\n\\]\nsuch that, with\n\\[\nv_{&lt;j}=v_{j-1}\\cdots v_1,\\qquad\nL_j=v_{&lt;j}Lv_{&lt;j}^{-1},\n\\]\nthe Levi subgroup \\(L_j\\) is standard and maximal proper in a standard Levi subgroup \\(H_j\\), and\n\\[\n\\Delta_{H_j}=\\Delta_{L_j}\\cup\\{\\alpha_j\\},\n\\qquad\nv_j=w_{H_j}w_{L_j}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inv}(v)\n=\n\\bigsqcup_{j=1}^t\nv_{&lt;j}^{-1}\\operatorname{Inv}(v_j).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nThe analogous partition holds for coroots.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(v\\neq1\\), choose a simple root \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(v\\alpha&lt;0\\). Such a root is outside \\(\\Delta_L\\), since \\(v\\Delta_L\\subseteq\\Delta_H\\). Let \\(H_1\\) be the standard Levi obtained by adjoining \\(\\alpha\\) to \\(\\Delta_L\\), and set\n\\[\nv_1=w_{H_1}w_L.\n\\]</p><p>Every positive root of \\(H_1\\) outside \\(L\\) has a strictly positive \\(\\alpha\\)-coefficient. Its image under \\(v\\) is a positive linear combination of \\(v\\alpha\\) and roots in \\(v\\Delta_L\\). The negative root \\(v\\alpha\\) has a strictly negative coefficient outside \\(v\\Delta_L\\); otherwise it would belong to the span of \\(v\\Delta_L\\), contradicting \\(\\alpha\\notin\\operatorname{span}(\\Delta_L)\\). This coefficient cannot be canceled by the other summands. The image root is therefore negative. Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Inv}(v_1)\n=\n\\Phi_{H_1}^+\\setminus\\Phi_L^+\n\\subseteq\\operatorname{Inv}(v).\n\\]\nConsequently, \\(v_1\\) is a right factor with additive length.</p><p>The element \\(v_1\\) carries \\(\\Delta_L\\) into the simple roots of \\(H_1\\). Replace \\(L\\) by \\(v_1Lv_1^{-1}\\), and \\(v\\) by \\(vv_1^{-1}\\), which still carries the new Levi simple system into \\(\\Delta_H\\). The length has strictly decreased, so iteration terminates.</p><p>The usual inversion-set identity for a product with additive lengths gives \\((4.4)\\). Applying the same argument to the dual root system gives the coroot version.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Standard intertwining operators and their Whittaker scalars factor according to Lemma \\(6.2\\), giving multiplicativity of local coefficients up to the units allowed in Proposition \\(6\\).", "statement": "<p>Multiplicativity and reduction to supercuspidal inducing data, from Section \\(4.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the local-coefficient setting of Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the reduction of inducing data, assume \\(P=MR\\) is maximal in \\(H\\), with omitted simple root \\(\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widetilde\\alpha\\in\\mathfrak a^*_{M,\\mathbb R}\\) be proportional to the character giving the determinant on \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(R)\\), normalized by\n  \\[\n  \\langle\\widetilde\\alpha,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=1.\n  \\]\n  Its pairing with every simple coroot of \\(M\\) is zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  \\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\n  =\n  \\bigoplus_{i\\geq1}V_i,\\qquad\n  V_i=\n  \\bigoplus_{\\langle\\widetilde\\alpha,\\beta^\\vee\\rangle=i}\n  \\widehat{\\mathfrak h}_{\\beta^\\vee},\n  \\]\n  and denote the action on \\(V_i\\) by \\(r_i\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Standard intertwining operators and their Whittaker scalars factor according to Lemma \\(6.2\\), giving multiplicativity of local coefficients up to the units allowed in Proposition \\(6\\).</p><p>For maximal \\(P\\), it suffices to establish the comparison \\((4.3)\\) on the relative line \\(z=s\\widetilde\\alpha\\), where its arguments are \\(is\\) and \\(1-is\\). Comparison for maximal parabolics with generic supercuspidal inducing data implies comparison for maximal parabolics with arbitrary generic inducing data.</p>", "proof": "<p>The standard intertwining integrals factor according to Lemma \\(6.2\\), first by Fubini's theorem in a convergence chamber and then by meromorphic continuation. At each step, normalized induction in stages identifies the elementary operator with the maximal-parabolic operator in \\(H_j\\). Taking Whittaker scalars and using Lemma \\(6.1\\) gives multiplicativity, up to the allowed units. This is the multiplicativity of Lomelí [19, Proposition \\(2.3\\)].</p><p>Every twist direction for maximal \\(P\\) is a sum of the relative direction \\(\\widetilde\\alpha\\) and a direction in \\(X^*(H)\\otimes\\mathbb C\\). A twist from \\(H\\) twists both sides of the intertwiner and changes its Whittaker scalar by at most a unit. On the dual side, it acts through \\(Z(\\widehat H)^\\circ\\) and acts trivially on \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\\). Thus it suffices to work on \\(z=s\\widetilde\\alpha\\).</p><p>By the subrepresentation theorem, \\(\\sigma\\) embeds in normalized induction in \\(M\\) from a supercuspidal representation \\(\\sigma_0\\) of a standard Levi subgroup \\(M_0\\subseteq M\\). Exactness and heredity of Whittaker coinvariants show that \\(\\sigma_0\\) is generic. They also show that the embedding induces an isomorphism on Whittaker lines, since both lines are one-dimensional. The same holds after every unramified twist.</p><p>The element \\(w=w_Hw_M\\) carries the simple roots of \\(M_0\\) into those of \\(H\\). In the induced realization from \\(M_0\\), its intertwining integral still uses exactly the roots outside \\(M\\) turned negative by \\(w\\): \\(w\\) preserves the positive system of \\(M\\) in its target Levi. Thus induction in stages identifies the intertwiner on \\(\\sigma\\) with the restriction of this intertwiner on the smaller inducing data. The Whittaker-line isomorphisms identify their local coefficients, up to units.</p><p>Apply Lemma \\(6.2\\) with \\(L=M_0\\), and write\n\\[\nM_j=w_{&lt;j}M_0w_{&lt;j}^{-1}.\n\\]\nAt step \\(j\\), the inducing representation is the transport of \\(\\sigma_0\\) to \\(M_j\\), and the twist is \\(w_{&lt;j}(s\\widetilde\\alpha)\\). Its relative coordinate in \\(H_j\\) is \\(c_js\\), where\n\\[\nc_j=\\langle w_{&lt;j}\\widetilde\\alpha,\\alpha_j^\\vee\\rangle&gt;0.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(w_{&lt;j}^{-1}\\alpha_j^\\vee\\) belongs to the original inversion set of coroots, whose elements are positive and outside \\(M\\). Pairing with \\(\\widetilde\\alpha\\) is strictly positive on this set. Every elementary coefficient therefore has a genuinely varying argument; the reduction does not restrict a meromorphic coefficient to an identically singular locus.</p><p>The same factorization decomposes the dual representations. Transport the nilradical Lie algebra for \\(M_j\\subset H_j\\) back by \\(w_{&lt;j}^{-1}\\). It is invariant under \\(\\widehat M_0\\), because the original algebra is invariant under \\(\\widehat M_j\\). The coroot partition \\((4.4)\\) identifies their direct sum with \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\\) restricted to \\(\\widehat M_0\\).</p><p>A step summand of relative degree \\(i_j\\) has twist exponent \\(c_ji_js\\). To see this, decompose \\(w_{&lt;j}\\widetilde\\alpha\\) into \\(c_j\\widetilde\\alpha_j\\) and a character direction from \\(H_j\\); the latter pairs to zero with every coroot in \\(H_j\\).</p><p>Compatibility of semisimple parameters with normalized induction, from Proposition \\(2\\), identifies \\(\\rho_\\sigma\\) with the parameter obtained from \\(\\rho_{\\sigma_0}\\). Taking monodromy zero, the preceding decomposition makes the proposed right side of \\((4.3)\\) the product of the proposed right sides for the elementary maximal parabolics. This proves the asserted reduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The comparison \\((4.3)\\) holds for these inducing representations.", "statement": "<p>The rank-one and principal-series comparisons, from Section \\(4.3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the maximal-parabolic setting and relative coordinate \\(s\\widetilde\\alpha\\) of Lemma \\(6.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume either that \\(M\\) is a torus, or that \\(\\sigma\\) is an irreducible generic constituent of a principal series of \\(M(k)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The comparison \\((4.3)\\) holds for these inducing representations. In particular, it holds for ramified principal-series constituents; no Iwahori-fixed hypothesis is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(M\\) is a torus, the derived root system of \\(H\\) has rank one. The local coefficient is the Tate gamma factor for the character obtained by pulling \\(\\sigma\\) back along the coroot. Tate's formula and local class field theory give\n\\[\nC_H(s\\widetilde\\alpha,\\sigma)\n\\doteq\n\\frac{L(1-s,r_1^\\vee\\circ\\rho_\\sigma)}\n     {L(s,r_1\\circ\\rho_\\sigma)}.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nThis calculation permits an arbitrary smooth inducing character; see Lomelí [19, Section \\(1.3\\)]. It also applies to the isogeny types occurring here. Pulling the integral back through the root homomorphism from \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) identifies the root groups and the unipotent integration, and the inducing character pulls back through the stated coroot. Extra central tori have no effect.</p><p>An irreducible generic constituent of a principal series can be embedded in a principal series, with a possibly Weyl-conjugate inducing character, by the subrepresentation and supercuspidal support theorems. The elementary factorization and reduction of Lemma \\(6.3\\), with \\(M_0=T\\), reduce its comparison to \\((4.6)\\).</p><p>This proves the comparison with monodromy zero. Proposition \\(3\\), applied to the relevant dual representations, permits any completion with the same Weil part.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( C_H(s\\widetilde\\alpha,\\sigma) \\doteq \\prod_i \\frac{L(1-is,r_i^\\vee\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma))} {L(is,r_i\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma))}. \\)", "statement": "<p>The supercuspidal comparison by globalization, from Section \\(4.4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the maximal-parabolic setting of Lemma \\(6.3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) be an irreducible generic supercuspidal representation of \\(M(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma)\\) be any Weil–Deligne completion of its semisimple parameter.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\nC_H(s\\widetilde\\alpha,\\sigma)\n\\doteq\n\\prod_i\n\\frac{L(1-is,r_i^\\vee\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma))}\n     {L(is,r_i\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma))}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First twist \\(\\sigma\\) so that its central character has finite order. By Proposition \\(1\\), \\(Z(M)\\) is a split torus. A smooth character of its maximal compact subgroup has finite image, and restriction\n\\[\nX^*(M)\\longrightarrow X^*(Z(M))\n\\]\nhas finite cokernel. Unramified twists can therefore adjust the finitely many uniformizer values to roots of unity. Twisting back shifts the relative parameter \\(s\\); the remaining twist direction is from \\(H\\) and behaves as in Lemma \\(6.3\\). Twist compatibility of semisimple parameters gives the identical shift on the proposed Galois expression.</p><p>Write \\(k=k_0((t))\\), put \\(K=k_0(t)\\), and let \\(v_0\\) be the rational place \\(t=0\\), so \\(K_{v_0}=k\\). Use constant split models of \\(H\\) and \\(M\\) over \\(K\\).</p><p>The finite-order central character extends to a finite-order automorphic character of \\(Z(M)(\\mathbb A_K)\\). For one split central factor, the idele-class group fits into the split exact sequence\n\\[\n1\\longrightarrow\n\\left(\\prod_x\\mathcal O_x^\\times\\right)/k_0^\\times\n\\longrightarrow K^\\times\\backslash\\mathbb A_K^\\times\n\\xrightarrow{\\deg}\\mathbb Z\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nThe degree-zero description follows from \\(\\operatorname{Pic}^0(\\mathbb P^1)=0\\), and the uniformizer idele at \\(v_0\\) splits the degree map.</p><p>Prescribe the given unit character at \\(v_0\\), cancel its restriction to \\(k_0^\\times\\) using a character of the residue field at infinity, and take trivial characters at the remaining unit groups. This defines a finite-order character of the left group in \\((4.7)\\). Give the degree generator the prescribed finite-order value of the local character on \\(t\\). The resulting automorphic character has the desired restriction at \\(v_0\\). Repeat for every split central factor.</p><p>Choose a nontrivial global additive character and the resulting generic character of the Borel unipotent of \\(M\\). Its local character at \\(v_0\\) is in the torus orbit for which \\(\\sigma\\) is generic. Gan–Lomelí's globalization theorem [13, Theorem \\(1.1\\)] produces a globally generic cuspidal representation \\(\\Pi\\) of \\(M(\\mathbb A_K)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\Pi_{v_0}\\simeq\\sigma,\\qquad\n\\Pi_x\\text{ is a principal-series constituent for every }x\\neq v_0,\n\\]\nwith the finite-order central character just constructed. The theorem preserves the prescribed unipotent period; for the Borel unipotent and a nondegenerate character this is global genericity. It imposes no restriction on depth or positive characteristic.</p><p>Choose an occurring global parameter \\(\\Sigma\\) as in Proposition \\(2\\). Enlarge a finite set \\(S\\) containing \\(v_0\\) so that normalized Satake matching and all unramified conditions for the crude functional equation hold outside \\(S\\). Lomelí's crude functional equation [19, Theorem \\(4.3\\)] is\n\\[\n\\prod_i\n\\frac{L^S(is,\\Pi,r_i)}\n     {L^S(1-is,\\Pi,r_i^\\vee)}\n\\doteq\n\\prod_{x\\in S}C_{H_x}(s\\widetilde\\alpha,\\Pi_x).\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nIn the cited theorem, the denominator is written with the contragredient \\(\\widetilde\\Pi\\) and \\(r_i\\). At an unramified place, contragredience inverts the Satake class up to the Levi Weyl group, so these Euler factors are exactly those displayed.</p><p>The same partial Euler products are the partial \\(L\\)-functions of \\(r_i\\circ\\Sigma\\). Let \\(\\Phi_x\\) be the local Weil–Deligne parameter obtained from \\(\\Sigma\\) at \\(x\\). The functional equation for a lisse sheaf on a curve, with ramified factors defined by inertia invariants, gives\n\\[\n\\prod_i\n\\frac{L^S(is,\\Pi,r_i)}\n     {L^S(1-is,\\Pi,r_i^\\vee)}\n\\doteq\n\\prod_{x\\in S}\\prod_i\n\\frac{L(1-is,r_i^\\vee\\circ\\Phi_x)}\n     {L(is,r_i\\circ\\Phi_x)}.\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nThis is the curve functional equation of Deligne [8, Sections \\(9\\)–\\(10\\)], obtained from the trace formula and duality for middle extensions; it does not require purity. To check the direction of the ratio, write the complete functional equation as\n\\[\nL(s,V)=\\epsilon(s,V)L(1-s,V^\\vee).\n\\]\nRemoving the Euler factors in \\(S\\) puts\n\\[\nL_x(1-s,V^\\vee)/L_x(s,V)\n\\]\non the right. The global epsilon factor is a nonzero constant times a monomial, and after \\(s\\mapsto is\\) is of the form suppressed by \\(\\doteq\\).</p><p>The \\(\\ell\\)-adic functional equation is an identity of rational functions, transported through the fixed coefficient identification. Its partial \\(L\\)-functions agree with the automorphic ones because their Euler factors agree outside \\(S\\). Its ramified factors are those of \\(\\Phi_x\\): the invariant stalk is the inertia-invariant kernel of monodromy, and Frobenius semisimplification does not change its determinant.</p><p>For every \\(x\\in S\\setminus\\{v_0\\}\\), \\(\\Pi_x\\) is generic and a principal-series constituent. Lemma \\(6.4\\) identifies its factor in \\((4.8)\\) with its factor in \\((4.9)\\), up to a unit. Proposition \\(2\\) identifies the Weil part of \\(\\Phi_x\\) with \\(\\rho_{\\Pi_x}\\), and Proposition \\(3\\) permits monodromy to be discarded in comparing the ratios.</p><p>Cancellation leaves\n\\[\nC_H(s\\widetilde\\alpha,\\sigma)\n\\doteq\n\\prod_i\n\\frac{L(1-is,r_i^\\vee\\circ\\Phi_{v_0})}\n     {L(is,r_i\\circ\\Phi_{v_0})}.\n\\]\nAt \\(v_0\\), the same compatibility and Proposition \\(3\\) replace \\(\\Phi_{v_0}\\) by \\((\\rho_\\sigma,0)\\), or by the specified completion \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_\\sigma)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "adjoint regularity for a generic Langlands quotient", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The Weil parameter satisfies \\(r\\simeq\\rho_\\tau\\), and \\( L(s,\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ(r,N_M)) \\) is regular at \\(s=1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a local function field of residue cardinality \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G/k\\) be split, adjoint, and absolutely simple.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P=MR\\) be a proper standard parabolic subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) be tempered on \\(M(k)\\), and let\n  \\[\n  \\nu\\in X^*(M)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb R\n  \\]\n  lie in the open positive chamber for \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\tau\\) be a generic irreducible representation that is the Langlands quotient of \\(I_P^G(\\sigma_\\nu)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a tempered completion \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_M)\\) supplied by Proposition \\(2\\), twist it by \\(\\nu\\), and include it in \\(\\widehat G\\). Write the resulting pair as \\((r,N_M)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use geometric Frobenius and the local-factor conventions of Proposition \\(3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Weil parameter satisfies \\(r\\simeq\\rho_\\tau\\), and\n\\[\nL(s,\\operatorname{Ad}\\circ(r,N_M))\n\\]\nis regular at \\(s=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Exactness and heredity of Whittaker coinvariants imply that \\(\\sigma\\) is generic. The assertion about \\(r\\) follows from normalized-induction and twist compatibility in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p>Let \\(A(z)\\) be the unnormalized long intertwiner on \\(I_P^G(\\sigma_z)\\). For tempered inducing data, \\(A(z)\\) is holomorphic at \\(\\nu\\), and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}A(\\nu)\n\\]\nis the Langlands quotient; see Renard [22, Lemme VII.4.1 and Théorème VII.4.2] and del Castillo–Henniart–Lomelí [7, Section \\(2.2\\)]. Since this image is generic, exactness of Whittaker coinvariants shows that the induced map on the two Whittaker lines is nonzero at \\(\\nu\\). Lemma \\(6.1\\) supplies algebraic trivializations with nonvanishing specializations. Thus \\(C_G(z,\\sigma)\\), the inverse of that scalar, is holomorphic and nonzero at \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>Decompose\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}=\\bigoplus_bV_b\n\\]\nunder \\(Z(\\widehat M)^\\circ\\), and write \\(r_b\\) for the actions.</p><p>For any algebraic representation of a tempered parameter, the Frobenius eigenvalues on the kernel of monodromy have absolute value at most \\(1\\). Indeed, a highest weight \\(m\\geq0\\) of the \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\) factor contributes \\(Q^{-m/2}\\) in \\((2.3)\\), and the commuting Weil action has eigenvalues of absolute value \\(1\\). Consequently, its local \\(L\\)-factor is regular and nonzero when the argument has positive real part.</p><p>Since \\(b(\\nu)&gt;0\\), every denominator in Proposition \\(6\\), using \\((\\rho_\\sigma,N_M)\\), is regular and nonzero at \\(z=\\nu\\). The product ratio is also regular and nonzero there. Local \\(L\\)-factors have no zeros, so none of the numerator factors\n\\[\nL(1-b(\\nu),r_b^\\vee\\circ(\\rho_\\sigma,N_M))\n\\]\ncan have a pole.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat G)\n=\n\\operatorname{Lie}(\\widehat M)\n\\oplus\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}\n\\oplus\\widehat{\\mathfrak n}^{-}.\n\\]\nThe Killing form identifies the last summand with the dual of the second. On the first summand the central twist is trivial, so its factor is regular at \\(1\\) by temperedness. The second contributes arguments \\(1+b(\\nu)&gt;0\\). The third contributes the arguments \\(1-b(\\nu)\\) just controlled by the numerator factors. Multiplication proves the assertion.</p>"}], "edges": [[4, 3, "part"], [5, 3, "part"], [6, 3, "part"], [11, 10, "part"], [12, 10, "part"], [13, 10, "part"], [14, 10, "part"], [15, 10, "part"]]});
